- Dengue outbreaks in Rio Grande do Sul (≈210,000 confirmed infections and 281 deaths) and Bahia (≈163,000 cases, 188 deaths) in 2024 illustrate climate-driven expansion of Aedes aegypti. A Fiocruz study (2006–2024) shows dengue epidemic microrregions rising from 110 to 292, now representing about 54% of Brazil, while 46% remain medium to low risk. Extreme temperature anomalies underpin this spread, with transmission unlikely to revert to zero. Oropouche fever surpassed 12,000 infections in 2025 across 23 states and the Federal District. Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome records 530 infections and 212 deaths since 2016; floods fuel leptospirosis and hepatitis A risk. Droughts and fires may drive rodent‑borne and fungal threats as temperatures rise. El Niño–related extremes are expected to 
- Dengue in the Americas drops 58% in 2026 through week 37, with 1,692,937 suspected cases, 523,102 lab-confirmed, 3,413 severe dengue (0.2%), and 571 deaths (case fatality 0.034%). Colombia reports the largest burden, with 83,905 suspected through week 38 and 28,677 confirmed; incidence 157.12 per 100,000 and 856 severe cases. Four dengue serotypes circulate nationwide (DENV-1–4), with DENV-3 predominant since late 2025, raising severe-disease risk upon reinfection. Colombia remains in outbreak status per the endemic channel; 16 departments (and cities like Cali, Cartagena, Barranquilla) show >30% increases. INS BES week 42 notes seven dengue deaths in that week; 94,618 cases in 2026 and 64 deaths overall. Barranquilla reports 2,895 cases; Qdenga vaccination is offered free to 9-year-olds. 
- Dengue in the Federal District, Brazil, enters high transmission risk as rains arrive. Through Oct 24, 2026, 12,130 suspected dengue cases were notified; 11,107 met case definitions, including 3,652 probable and 231 confirmed, with 16 showing warning signs; no deaths or severe dengue reported to week 42. Laboratory surveillance shows circulation of DENV-1, DENV-2, and DENV-3, with DENV-3 predominating. The Sudoeste Health Region records the most probable cases (913), followed by Leste (608) and Oeste (564); however, incidence per 100,000 people is highest in Leste (164.2), then Sul (142.3) and Oeste (133). The rains combined with high temperatures accelerate Aedes aegypti development, increasing seasonal transmission. Preventive focus includes vector control and public health surveillance;
